{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>ויתן [and he gave].</b> Some interpret concerning the dead of exile, and some say that the word במותיו is from the root of ואתה על במותימו תדרך (Deuteronomy 33:29), and the meaning is a structure that is placed over the grave, and so במותיו is like 'his grave':" "<b>ואת עשיר [and with the rich].</b> Like ואת רשעים, and the meaning is the nations who are rich compared to Israel. And the correct interpretation in my eyes is that the meaning of the verse is that Israel was in such distress in exile that he wished to die among the nations, like תמות נפשי עם פלשתים (Judges 16:30). And Scripture says ויתן על מחשבתו, like וילחן בישראל (Joshua 24:9) על בלק. And the proof that this is from abundant distress is that he said על לא חמס עשה, for the nations afflict Israel gratuitously, not on account of a deed they did, and not on account of an evil word they spoke — which is correctly interpreted in two directions. And if a disputant objects: is not במות unchanged in the phrase על במותימו? Why does it change in the word במותיו? — one may answer that this word comes in two patterns, like סריסי פרעה (Genesis 40:7) and סריסי המלך (Esther 6:14)."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 53, :notes {0 "Resolved מפרשים על מתי גלות as 'interpret concerning the dead of exile'; the syntax is compressed and the referent is Israel's dead in exile.", 1 "The phrase ואת רשעים alludes to Isaiah 53:9 ('ואת עשיר במותיו'); the parallel word in the verse is רשעים. In וילחם בישראל על בלק, Ibn Ezra cites Joshua 24:9 to show a prepositional על construction parallel to ויתן על מחשבתו."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:59:27.093311064Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}